    mainDescription:
      "<p>Industrial real estate covers a range of property types, including warehouse and distribution buildings, manufacturing facilities, and flex space combining office and warehouse or light industrial use. San Francisco Bay Area industrial property has seen sustained demand tied to logistics, last mile distribution, and the broader technology and life science sectors that require specialized manufacturing or research and development space, which has made industrial one of the more sought after commercial asset classes for 1031 exchange investors in recent years.</p><h3>What Drives Industrial Property Demand</h3><p>Warehouse and distribution demand is generally tied to e-commerce fulfillment and regional logistics networks, with properties located near major transportation corridors and population centers commanding premium rents due to the value of shorter last mile delivery times. Manufacturing and specialized industrial space, including life science and biotech focused buildings common in parts of the Bay Area, generally requires more tenant specific improvements and carries higher retrofit costs if a tenant vacates, since the next tenant may need a different configuration. Flex space, combining office and industrial or warehouse function, offers more tenant flexibility but can also see more turnover as tenant space needs change over time, which is a consideration for San Francisco, CA investors evaluating the trade off between specialized, higher rent industrial space and more flexible, but potentially higher turnover, flex space.</p><h3>Industrial Property in a 1031 Exchange</h3><p>Industrial property held for investment or business use generally qualifies as like kind real property for a 1031 exchange, and it is a common destination for San Francisco, CA investors exchanging out of an aging retail or office property, since industrial space has generally seen more resilient tenant demand and rent growth in recent years compared to some traditional retail and office categories. Investors should evaluate a candidate industrial property's tenant industry concentration, clear height and loading configuration relative to current logistics standards, and any environmental considerations tied to prior industrial or manufacturing use, since environmental due diligence is a more significant part of industrial underwriting than for most other commercial property types.</p><p>Financing for industrial property is generally available from conventional commercial lenders, with underwriting focused on the tenant's lease term and credit quality, the building's physical specifications relative to current market standards, such as clear height and loading dock configuration, and the local supply and demand balance for industrial space in the target market. We help San Francisco, CA investors evaluate industrial acquisition candidates, coordinate environmental and property condition due diligence, work with lenders familiar with industrial underwriting, and structure the exchange to meet the identification and closing deadlines.</p>",
